L(314, "  - **What \"no value\" means,**", r'''
  - **What concluding without acceptance means,** when the cause is not rule-level. **The canary:** nothing under this amendment proceeds; D07's owner review follows the canary's attempts (D113), as the plan has it for its own re-canary (lines 286–289), and a further native CHORUS pilot needs a prospective amendment. **The AI_READI pilot:** AI_READI leaves scope as infeasible, reported "out of scope (pilot)" as a feasibility result, and CHORUS's pilot alone sets the floor (plan step 8, lines 307–312). **A re-run pilot** takes the place of the pilot it re-runs. A CHORUS re-run that concludes without acceptance leaves CHORUS with no fraction: the floor is not registered, and no production cell registers until a prospective amendment says how it is set (NP1.5). An AI_READI re-run that concludes without acceptance takes AI_READI out of scope, with the earlier reading reported.
  - **Only an ending without acceptance removes AI_READI.** An accepted AI_READI pilot whose coverage reading is missing, has a zero denominator or is under 1/20 stays in scope, and the floor is not registered until a prospective amendment says how it is set (NP1.5). An instrument case stays pending.
  - **Usage can decide this.** Two usage stops conclude a pilot without acceptance: row 4 allows one contingency, and the second ending concludes it. Under the production limit set a run is longer and crosses more usage windows (NP1.7). So the account's capacity can take AI_READI out of scope, and with it the largest three-replicate project out of the main result. That is a feasibility result and is reported as one. NP1b may not use usage readings to change it.
  - **These consequences are not the same as a production cell's.** A pilot that concludes without acceptance for a cause that is not rule-level takes AI_READI out of scope whole. The same endings in a production cell of CM4AI or VOICE leave that project in scope and in K_native, with missing cells or measurements (NP1.2). v5 removed the automatic cancellation after a first backstop end; it did not remove this difference.
''')
L(315, "  - **Giving no further word.**", r'''
  - **Giving no further word.** Every generation needs its own word (D22), so no rule here can compel one. But a withheld word concludes nothing. The pilot stays pending, the floor is not registered, and no production cell registers: production does not start on a CHORUS-only floor because a word was withheld. v5 let the owner record that no further word would come, which concluded the pilot. After a rejected AI_READI attempt, with whatever its saved stages show, its coverage readings included, that could keep the floor at CHORUS's known value by giving up AI_READI. **Taking AI_READI out of scope after a rejected attempt, without its second attempt, takes a ratified change to this amendment.** Under NP1.5 such a change applies only to cells registered after it, defines a new prospective stratum that names its pilots before any of them runs, and may not use a native result to choose. The same holds for a re-run pilot and for the canary: with no further word each stays pending.
''')
L(318, "  - **The replacement pilot** [proposed, NP1.10(aj)]", r'''
  - **A re-run pilot** [proposed, NP1.10(al)] exists only where NP1.5 requires one: for CHORUS, where the canary-first condition failed; and for either pilot, where the equivalence test finds its source not equivalent to the source to be pinned. v6 called the CHORUS case "the replacement pilot". It is one native run of that pilot's project, n=1, never a replicate and never one of this amendment's cells, with its own label, registration, two reviews and word. It registers on the commit the floor's registration will pin, under the production limit set (NP1.10(ab)), with the floor pending as the canary's was; D109 set that state for the canary, and applying it here is part of that item. It runs before the floor's registration, whichever pilot it re-runs. It is judged by rows B0–B7, with B5 reported and UNMEASURABLE. Its two attempts are its own. It takes the place of the pilot it re-runs in the table above, and a rule-level rejection of it ends the native shared-v1 condition. There is at most one re-run per pilot (NP1.5).
''')

