L(372, "    - **Why the canary can still be CHORUS's pilot.**", r'''
    - **A declared transfer assumption, not an equivalence.** The canary runs under $90 and 21,600 s, and its reading calibrates a floor for cells that run under $400 and 43,200 s. The two sets accept different sets of possible attempts. A CHORUS attempt that finishes after seven hours, or at a cost count above $90, can be accepted under the production set and cannot under the canary's. Conditioning on acceptance therefore selects differently under the two sets, even though the model reads neither limit. The plan says the same of any change in stopping: it changes "which calls and cells complete" (lines 1221–1224). So the canary can be CHORUS's pilot only on the assumption that a CHORUS run accepted under the canary's set reads coverage as one accepted under the production set would. This amendment declares that assumption. It does not test it. Each pilot's limit set is reported beside the floor's value. What is checked is narrower: that the two sets differ in nothing the model reads beyond the permitted identity fields, before the canary's first coded word (NP1.5).
''')
L(374, "    - **What a longer run changes.**", r'''
    - **What a longer run changes.** A 43,200 s run crosses more five-hour usage windows than a 21,600 s run, and draws more on the seven-day window. The admission terms (NP1.10(ac)) and the usage-stop class (NP1.6, row 4) already cover a mid-run exhaustion, and N1.5 says one is possible whatever the admission reading. **A usage stop is more likely under the production set, and for a pilot it has a scope consequence:** two usage stops conclude the AI_READI pilot without acceptance, and AI_READI then leaves scope (NP1.6). The stand-down each attempt asks for doubles (below).
''')
L(375, "  - **A: one set for every run, the canary included:**", "  - **A (offered, not taken):** one set for every run, the canary included: a $400 guard and a 43,200 s deadline. The code would be simplest, two constants, and the canary and production would not differ at all. But it would change two of amendment N1's values, its item (p) and the guard D110 decided, so amendment N1 would have been revised and balloted again before the canary.")
L(376, "  - **C: the canary's terms for every run,**", "  - **C (offered, not taken):** the canary's terms for every run, as v5 had it. No code would change. On the plan's estimates three projects are expected to end on the guard and one is borderline. A project that needs other terms would then need source support and a later amendment, and its cells would be a new stratum with a pilot of its own.")
