L(441, "Each letter is one decision,", r'''
Each letter is one decision, and a "yes" adopts exactly the value given. The cells themselves are D135's scope and are not a lettered item. "Amendment N1's item" always means a letter of N1, never one of these. A note after an item says how it differs from v6; an item with no note is unchanged from v6. The letters are v6's, and (al) and (am) are new.

**Answered in advance.** On 2026-11-12 the owner answered three items and gave one direction (D140, D141, D143, D145; Authority): (r) at-receipt, (n) keep, (ab) B, and (am) delegated starts allowed. They are marked "Answered" below. Their written alternatives are kept as the record of what was offered. Ratification records them as answered and does not ask them again. Those answers did not ratify this amendment: the record says "no ratification of amendment NP1, which is still in review".

**Ratification mechanics for (ak).** A "no" to (ak) holds every registration under this amendment until a redraft of (ak) is ratified. This sentence is not part of item (ak), so a "no" to (ak) does not remove it.
''')
L(446, "- **Production registrations wait for every analysis-defining item that is still open:**", r'''
- **Production registrations wait for every analysis-defining item that is still open:** the eligibility rule (m), K_native (k), the role (i), the comparator (l), the predictions' status ((p), (q)), the native/API contrast (ag) and the source rule (al). The other analysis-defining items, (j), (n) and (o), carry written alternatives, so a "no" settles them at once.
- **No redraft may use a native result.** From the canary's first coded word to the end of production, a redraft may not use any native quality value or usage reading to choose a project, a rule or a threshold, and its header records what of the native outcomes its author, each reviewer and the owner had seen (NP1.5).
''')
L(447, "- **Nine items carry a complete written alternative:**", "- **Nine items carry a complete written alternative:** (e), (g), (h), (j), (n), (o), (r), (ab) and (aj). Three of them are answered: (n), (r) and (ab). For the others a \"no\" adopts the alternative as written, and nothing is redrafted. (aj)'s is fixed: the canary's reading is not a floor input, and CHORUS's pilot is run again.")
L(448, "- **No \"no\" prescribes new engineering.**", "- **No \"no\" prescribes new engineering.** (ab) was the one item whose written alternatives differed in code, and it is answered (D143).")
