L(456, "| (k) |", "| (k), (al) | the floor's registration, which pins the production source and fixes K_native, and so every re-run pilot's registration and every production registration |")
L(464, "- **(a)**", "- **(a)** Scope and order for the native arm: whole projects only, in the plan's order. At NP1b a project is in scope if and only if it passes NP1.2's inputs test on the manifest NP1.2 names for it: the study's source manifest for its five projects, and Kids First's own tracked manifest for Kids First. One that fails is \"not planned (NP1b: inputs)\". No usage reading, number of weeks or quality value decides scope at NP1b. AI_READI can also leave when its pilot concludes without acceptance; a withheld word removes nothing, and neither does an accepted pilot with no usable fraction (NP1.2, NP1.6). *Changed: the manifests are named; \"concludes without acceptance\".*")
L(466, "- **(c)**", "- **(c)** Kids First has one descriptive cell, run last under the plan's conditions, if it passes (a)'s test on its own tracked manifest. That test has not been run. It is the largest input, and the plan does not price its run (NP1.2). *Corrected: a tracked source manifest declares it.*")
L(469, "- **(f)**", "- **(f)** Every pilot has at most two attempts and concludes by NP1.6's table, with the scope consequence that table gives when a pilot concludes without acceptance. With no further word a pilot stays pending, and the floor is not registered. A re-run pilot takes the place of the pilot it re-runs (NP1.6). *Reworded.*")
S(471, " *Changed: the precedence sentence.*", "")
L(481, "- **(n)**", "- **(n)** **Answered: keep (D141).** The incomplete capture retains the current full and core records with their sha256, as members of its snapshot, as an engineering prerequisite of any pilot, so that a stopped or rejected attempt can have an eligible record. It depends on (m). D141 takes the change once its review finding #6016 is repaired. The alternative that was offered, and not taken: the native eligible set limited to attempts whose recorder ran (NP1.4). *Answered.*")
L(487, "- **(r)**", "- **(r)** **Answered: at-receipt (D140).** The floor's basis is D37's at-receipt coverage. A registered numeric floor declares `basis: receipted_part`, and one without it is refused; a pending registration is unchanged and may carry the at-receipt counters as a diagnostic reading. The code change is in the source the canary's registration pins, or, where the canary is not the production pilot, in the pinned production source. The basis does not change afterwards in this condition. The alternative that was offered, and not taken: the final-record basis (NP1.5). *Answered, in D140's form; the second branch added.*")
L(488, "- **(s)**", "- **(s)** The floor rule: the lowest of the eligible native pilots' at-receipt fractions, each read once from the final-stage check, rounded down to a multiple of 1/20 and capped at 19/20. The lowest value that can be registered is 1/20. The floor is not registered, and no production cell registers, while a required pilot is pending, while an instrument finding on the two readings is open, or where an accepted pilot has no usable fraction. In the last case the pilot's project stays in scope, and a prospective amendment says how the floor is set (NP1.5). *Changed.*")
L(489, "- **(t)**", "- **(t)** The registered floor gates production cells at the final stage only: N1.4(B)'s row B5 reads the final-stage check on (r)'s basis, and the reading at receipt completion is diagnostic and stops nothing. A floor failure is an attempt whose final authoritative assessment has `state: failed`, from a measurable ratio below the registered threshold. A pending, unavailable, unmeasurable or not-applicable assessment is not a floor failure. Pilots keep B5 reported (NP1.5, NP1.6). *Changed: the definition.*")
L(492, "- **(u)**", "- **(u)** N1.4(B)'s rows B0–B7 judge the AI_READI pilot, a re-run pilot and every production cell, with B0 read for production as the receipts gate under the structural floors and the registered floor (NP1.6). *Reworded.*")
L(495, "- **(x)**", "- **(x)** The plan's cause classes, precedence and next actions, unchanged, with NP1.6's native readings: the termination mechanism is recorded separately and never decides the class; the canary's endings are classed the same way for this amendment's purposes; a stopped attempt's \"final records\" are its eligible record and core; B6 reads every attempt whose saved capture pins a full or core record, and for the canary it does so before NP1b; a manual stop takes the class of its recorded reason (NP1.6). *Changed: the deadline for the canary.*")
L(499, "- **(z)**", "- **(z)** The staged procedure: no pilot or production registration under this amendment before the canary's acceptance record is signed; B6's reading of every canary attempt that kept a record before NP1b; then the measured-usage packet, then NP1b, then any pending pilot, then the comparison of each pilot with the source to be pinned and any re-run, then the floor's registration, each reviewed and ratified before the registrations that depend on it. NP1b applies rules, changes none and takes no number from the owner; computing the floor by (s) is an application (NP1.5, NP1.7). *Changed.*")
L(501, "- **(ab)**", "- **(ab)** **Answered: B (D143).** Runtime terms: two registered sets and nothing else. The canary keeps amendment N1's terms, a $90 guard and a 21,600 s deadline. Every pilot after the canary and every production cell runs under one production set, a $400 guard and a 43,200 s deadline. The source is changed before the canary to accept exactly these two sets, with no project named in code. The canary's reading calibrates the production floor under a declared transfer assumption, and each pilot's limit set is reported beside the floor. The alternatives that were offered, and not taken: A, one set of $400 and 43,200 s for every run including the canary; C, the canary's terms for every run (NP1.7). *Answered; the transfer assumption added.*")
L(502, "- **(ac)**", "- **(ac)** The admission bounds, overage disabled, the freshness terms, the keep-awake policy, the capture rules, the context mode and the quiet account, each decided or ratified for the canary, apply unchanged to every pilot and production attempt. One attempt at a time follows from the quiet account. The guard and the deadline are (ab)'s (NP1.7). *Reworded.*")
L(503, "- **(ad)**", "- **(ad)** One procedure: every production cell runs the pinned production source. After the floor's registration, a change to the native runner or to anything the model reads is compared with that source by (al)'s equivalence test, and the comparison is not optional. A change that fails it, which a change to admission, retry, stopping, selection or retention always does, is a new stratum under its own prospective amendment, and never moves this stratum's floor or K_native. A change that passes it stays in the stratum on D35's terms: the byte comparison, and one diagnostic canary that the owner separately authorizes (NP1.7). *Changed.*")
L(510, "- **(ai)**", "- **(ai)** No cell of the API comparison set and nothing on NP1.9's deferred list is a prerequisite for a native pilot or production cell. Amendment N1's item (y) was answered yes (D138), so the branch for a \"no\" does not apply (NP1.8, NP1.9). *Reworded.*")
L(513, "- **(aj)**", "- **(aj)** Canary first: the canary's coverage reading can be a floor input only if this amendment was ratified, by this file's sha256 with a recorded answer to every item, before the canary's first coded word. That is judged once, at that word, and nothing later changes it: a later change to this amendment applies only to cells registered after it, and a later change of procedure is a new prospective stratum that names its pilots before any of them runs. Where the condition failed, the canary's reading is never a floor input, and CHORUS's pilot is run again on the production source under (al). A no means the same: the canary's reading is not a floor input and CHORUS's pilot is run again; nothing is redrafted (NP1.5, NP1.6, NP1.7). *Changed: v6's second condition is now (al).*")
L(516, "- **(ak)**", r'''
- **(ak)** What a "no" means: it returns the item for redrafting, except for the nine items that carry a written alternative; a redraft is ratified before the first registration whose outcome it governs, as the table above names it; production registrations wait for every unresolved analysis-defining item; and from the canary's first coded word to the end of production no redraft or change uses a native quality value or usage reading to choose a project, a rule or a threshold (NP1.10, NP1.5). *Changed: the bar on native results.*

**Source and starts**
- **(al)** Which source a pilot's reading must come from. The production source is pinned once, as one commit, at the floor's registration. Each pilot, the canary and the AI_READI pilot alike, is compared with it by the equivalence test NP1.5 fixes: model-facing bytes identical apart from a closed list of identity fields, and the acceptance and attempt rules the same apart from the registered limit set, as both reviewers confirm. Equivalent: the pilot's reading stands and the pilot is not run again. Not equivalent: the pilot is run again once on the pinned source before the floor is registered, with the reason for the source change recorded first as a filed defect or a ratified prerequisite, and the earlier reading always reported beside the new one. The test decides: nobody declines it, and nobody orders a re-run it does not require. The two limit sets are checked against each other, by part 1 of the test, before the canary's first coded word (NP1.5, NP1.6, NP1.7). *New: it replaces v6's second condition of (aj).*
- **(am)** **Answered: allowed (D145).** Delegated starts for every pilot and production attempt: after giving an attempt's word, the owner may direct the lead to start that attempt's sequence, within the limits of amendment N1's items (z) and (aa), the direction naming that attempt's registration. Every attempt still needs its own word (D22) (NP1.7). *New.*
''')

